Title:
Avred Update: Advances in Reversing Microsoft Defender Signature Format

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This post summarizes new reverse-engineering findings about Microsoft Defender’s signature database format and explains how they clarify Avred’s detection behavior. It describes how Defender signatures are composed of multiple weighted sub-rules and a score threshold, where detections trigger only when the accumulated weight exceeds the threshold—explaining “non-dominant” vs “dominant” matches. It also highlights that signatures are largely byte-exact but support several wildcard/placeholder encodings that behave like length-bounded gaps or regex-like patterns. These wildcard capabilities explain why simple “signature breaking” via NOP/register changes may fail when rules intentionally wildcard those bytes. The content is most useful to red teamers, malware analysts, and detection engineers trying to understand Defender’s matching logic and why certain mutations evade or fail. It’s interesting because it provides a more accurate mental model of Defender signatures beyond naive fixed-string matching.

Technical Focus:
- Microsoft Defender signature DB structure (sub-rules, weights, thresholds)
- Detection scoring / threshold-based rule triggering
- Wildcard encodings (90 01/02/03/04/05) and their semantics
- YARA-equivalent representations of Defender match logic
- Windows Defender kernel scanning component (WdFilter.sys minifilter)

Use Cases:
- Designing more realistic AV evasion/mutation strategies that account for weighted sub-rules
- Explaining inconsistent detection results when testing payload variants against Defender
- Building tooling to parse/translate Defender signatures into analyst-friendly formats (e.g., YARA-like)
- Creating better defensive signatures by understanding robust wildcarding patterns
- Researching Defender’s on-access scanning pipeline via WdFilter.sys behavior

Keywords:
Microsoft Defender, Windows Defender, signature database, AV signatures, reverse engineering, avred, WdFilter.sys, minifilter driver, kernel-mode scanning, sub-rules, weighted rules, score threshold, dominant match, non-dominant match, wildcards, byte patterns, YARA, regex-like patterns, malware detection logic